course: dictionary
lesson: 14998
title: Repute: what people think
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Repute is a formal word for what people generally think of someone or something. It is another word for reputation. «The café is of good repute, so people queue outside every morning.» The queue tells you that many people think the café is good.
explain: You will often find repute in set phrases. «Of good repute» means people think well of it. «Of ill repute» means it has a bad name. Look at the words around the phrase: if the sentence says people warned you away, the place is probably of ill repute.
explain: Repute can also mean how you know someone. «I know the coach only by repute; everyone says he is kind.» Here by repute means you have heard about him from other people, but you have not met him yourself. Context shows you which meaning you need.
fact: Repute means what people generally think of someone or something.
fact: Repute is a formal word, and reputation means the same thing.
fact: «Of good repute» means people think well of someone or something.
fact: «Of ill repute» means something has a bad name.
fact: «By repute» means known from what other people say, not from meeting or seeing.
exercise: «The little café is of good repute, so people queue outside every morning.» What does repute mean here? | A kind of coffee | *What people generally think of the café | The number of tables inside | A loud noise from the kitchen
exercise: «The shop was of ill repute after it sold broken toys.» What does «of ill repute» suggest? | It was closed for lunch | It was brand new | It was very large | *It had a bad name
exercise: «I know the coach only by repute; everyone says he is kind.» Find and copy the one word that means reputation. | everyone | coach | kind | *repute
test: «The scientist was of high repute, and many people trusted her ideas.» What does «by repute» mean in this phrase: «I know her by repute»? | *Known from what other people say | Known by sight across the street | Known by heart | Known by touch
test: Which phrase means that a place has a bad name? | of good repute | by repute | *of ill repute | of great size
exam: Ravi says the bakery is «of good repute» and Sam says it is «of ill repute». What does this show? | They both agree the bakery is brand new | Ravi is the baker | The bakery is closed for the day | *They disagree about what people think of the bakery
